%I A397279 #36 Aug 02 2026 18:22:12
%S A397279 10,36,54,136,204,238,528,792,924,990,2080,3120,3640,3900,4030,8256,
%T A397279 12384,14448,15480,15996,16254,32896,49344,57568,61680,63736,64764,
%U A397279 65278,131328,196992,229824,246240,254448,258552,260604,261630,524800,787200,918400,984000,1016800,1033200,1041400,1045500,1047550
%N A397279 Numbers whose binary expansion is of the form 1^i 0^j 1^i 0^j with i,j >= 1.
%C A397279 These are binary squares of the form ww for which the repeated block w has exactly two runs, where a run is a maximal consecutive block of equal bits. Since the binary expansion of a positive integer begins with 1, such a block is necessarily of the form w = 1^i 0^j with i,j >= 1.
%C A397279 If the terms are arranged in rows by s=i+j, row s has s-1 terms, corresponding to i=1,...,s-1 and j=s-i.
%C A397279 For a term x=T(i,j), v_2(x)=j and the binary length of x is 2*(i+j). Thus i and j can be recovered uniquely from x.
%C A397279 The counting function satisfies #{a(n) <= x} = (log_2(x))^2/8 + O(log(x)).
%F A397279 Let T(i,j) = 2^j*(2^i-1)*(2^(i+j)+1), i,j >= 1. The sequence is the triangle T(i,j) read by increasing i+j and then increasing i.
%F A397279 For n >= 1, let s = ceiling((1+sqrt(8*n+1))/2), i = n - binomial(s-1,2), and j = s-i.
%F A397279 a(n) = 2^j*(2^i-1)*(2^s+1).
%F A397279 Within row s, T(i+1,s-i-1)-T(i,s-i) = 2^(s-i-1)*(2^s+1), for 1 <= i < s-1.
%e A397279 204 is a term because 204=(11001100)_2 is the concatenation of the block 1100 with itself, and 1100 has exactly two runs, namely 11 and 00.
%e A397279 153=(10011001)_2 is a binary square (A020330), but it is not a term because its repeated block 1001 has three runs, namely 1, 00, and 1.
%e A397279 Triangle T(n,k) begins:
%e A397279       10;
%e A397279       36,     54;
%e A397279      136,    204,    238;
%e A397279      528,    792,    924,    990;
%e A397279     2080,   3120,   3640,   3900,   4030;
%e A397279     8256,  12384,  14448,  15480,  15996,  16254;
%e A397279    32896,  49344,  57568,  61680,  63736,  64764,  65278;
%e A397279   131328, 196992, 229824, 246240, 254448, 258552, 260604, 261630;
%e A397279   ...
%e A397279 Triangle T(n,k) written in base 2 begins:
%e A397279           1010;
%e A397279         100100,       110110;
%e A397279       10001000,     11001100,     11101110;
%e A397279     1000010000,   1100011000,   1110011100,   1111011110;
%e A397279   100000100000, 110000110000, 111000111000, 111100111100, 111110111110;
%e A397279   ...
%p A397279 T:= (n,k) -> (2^k-1)*2^(n+1-k)*(2^(n+1)+1);
%p A397279 seq(seq(T(n,k), k=1..n), n=1..9);  # _Alois P. Heinz_, Jul 27 2026
%o A397279 (Python)
%o A397279 def a(n):
%o A397279     s = 2
%o A397279     while n > s*(s-1)//2:
%o A397279         s += 1
%o A397279     i = n - (s-2)*(s-1)//2
%o A397279     j = s - i
%o A397279     return 2**j * (2**i - 1) * (2**s + 1)
%o A397279 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 46)])
%Y A397279 Subsequence of A020330, A121016, and A164709.
%Y A397279 Main diagonal gives A170940(n+1).
%Y A397279 First column gives A007582(n+1).
%K A397279 base,nonn,tabl,easy
%O A397279 1,1
%A A397279 _Takayuki Kuriyama_, Jul 27 2026
